  "query_preview": "..Mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies across southeast \nArizona with above normal temperatures continuing as broad ridging \naloft remains in control. Moisture levels are still relatively \nelevated for this time of year with surface dewpoints in the mid 50s \nto low 60s resulting in some cumulus clouds. These won't really \ndevelop into anything in terms of precipitation chances as very dry \nair and subsidence dominates above 500 mb. There is a weak \ndisturbance/upper low within the broader ridging that will move \ntowards the White Mountains from the north to result in a slight \nchance (10 to 20 percent) of showers and thunderstorms both \nWednesday and Thursday afternoon. The rest of southeast Arizona \nwill remain dry with continued above normal temperatures.  \n\nA fairly benign weather pattern will continue Thursday and Friday \nwith an uptick in easterly winds. For this weekend, our focus will \nturn towards the tropics as Hurricane Rachel (well west of southern \nBaja) makes an abrupt 90 degree recurvature to the northeast as a \nstrong trough approaches the west coast. Latest NHC forecast shows \nRachel rapidly becoming post-tropical on Saturday, shortly after it \nmakes its turn to the northeast. There is still some spread in the \nensemble guidance on where the remnants of Rachel will end up with \nthe most likely trajectory now being towards Baja California or even \nas far north southern California. Regardless, the remains of Rachel \nwill help send above normal moisture levels into the southwest US \nincluding our forecast area. These above normal moisture levels in \ncombination with forcing and lift from troughing aloft will result \nin an extended period of rain chances. While PoPs (around 20%) get \nintroduced as early as Saturday afternoon, recent trends point \ntowards a period of 20% to 50% rain chances (with potential for a \nfew thunderstorms as well) from Sunday through Tuesday. Most of the \nensembles keep QPF in our area at reasonable levels (0.25\" to 0.75\") \nduring this time frame but there are a few outliers on the higher \nside (1 to 2\"+) that shows the higher end/worst case potential. \nStill have time to sort these details out and have a better handle \non the interaction of the trough with remnants from Rachel and where \nthe heaviest precipitation will line up. Otherwise, starting Sunday, \ncooler temperatures will move in as well with highs below normal \nearly next week.",
